<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mary I of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1516–1558</span> · Also known as Mary Tudor, Bloody Mary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mary I was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Ireland from 1553 to 1558, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> and Catherine of Aragon. She restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> in 1554 and had about 280 Protestants burned for heresy. Her reign saw the plantation of Laois and Offaly in Ireland and, in 1558, the loss of Calais, England's last possession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-i-of-england.jpg" alt="Mary I of England" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonis Mor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthonis_Mor_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
